2. Safe Setup Before Prompting Copilot
 * Duplicate Report landing Page in Power BI Desktop: Right-click Report landing Page → Duplicate Page, rename the copy (for example, Attendance - Feature View), and press Ctrl + S so Power BI creates a valid page folder. Leave Report landing Page, Detailed Info, and Glossary untouched.
 * Create a safety commit in VS Code terminal:
   git add . ; git commit -m "baseline before new feature page"

 * Keep existing measures untouched: Any new KPIs you add for your feature should go into a new measure table (such as _Feature_Measures) or use a clear prefix while referencing existing measures in Calculations ([Total Attendance], [PY Attendance], [Attendance Growth %]) rather than rewriting them.
3. VS Code Copilot Prompt (Reference Existing Metrics + Build New Feature + Explain Everything)
Paste this into VS Code Copilot Chat (Interactive mode) once your duplicate page is saved and the folder is trusted:
> Do NOT use Computer Use. Keep Report landing Page, Detailed Info, and Glossary completely untouched, and do NOT modify or overwrite any existing measures in Calculations or any existing columns/tables (Calendar, Glossary, Test Table).
> Step 1 — Inspect & Document Baseline First:
> Using the Power BI Authoring MCP server and the files in Attendance.Report and Attendance.SemanticModel, inspect the existing semantic model, existing DAX measures in Calculations, relationships, and the visuals on Report landing Page. Summarize the exact existing measures and data model structure first before making edits.
> Step 2 — Build Feature on the New Page Only:
> Edit ONLY the duplicated page (Attendance - Feature View) and create a new measure table named _Feature_Measures (or add new measures there) that references existing measures ([Total Attendance], [PY Attendance], [Attendance Growth %]) rather than replacing them.
> Focus the new feature on a high-value operational question from the dataset—such as rolling weekly/period momentum, variance vs. prior year (Total Attendance - PY Attendance), and period-over-period consistency across Calendar periods (Year, Quarter, Month, Week Number)—and add focused visuals on Attendance - Feature View matching the project's existing style without adding filterConfig or TopN blocks.
> Step 3 — Teammate Handover Explanation:
> At the end, provide a clear, structured explanation covering:
>  * What existing measures/tables were referenced (and what was left untouched).
>  * Every new DAX measure created and the business logic behind it.
>  * Every visual/file added or modified on Attendance - Feature View (including any caveats around Test Table or schema assumptions) so I can explain it directly to my teammates.
> 
4. Quick Checklist for Explaining to Your Teammates Afterward
When you walk your team through the finished change, structure your explanation in four short beats:
 * Baseline preserved: Report landing Page, Detailed Info, Glossary, and existing measures in Calculations stayed intact.
 * What was added: The new page (Attendance - Feature View), new measures in _Feature_Measures, and the new feature visuals.
 * How the math works: Which existing base measures ([Total Attendance], [PY Attendance], [Attendance Growth %]) and Calendar fields (Year, Quarter, Month, Week Number) drive the new feature metrics.
 * Open items to check together: Any assumptions made around Test Table, KPI goal lines, or refresh behavior (Last Refresh / Last Refreshed on).
